WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:03.460
She composed for the legendary Aretha Franklin.

00:00:03.460 --> 00:00:06.640
Beyoncé called her “one of the best rappers
ever.”

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:08.730
And OutKast even opened for her.

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:11.849
We’re talking about no one other than Ms.
Lauryn Hill.

00:00:11.849 --> 00:00:14.380
Her talent is undeniable.

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:18.310
Rolling Stone named her one of the greatest
singers of all time, and Time Magazine dubbed

00:00:18.310 --> 00:00:20.100
her the “Queen of Hip Hop.”

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:24.340
But she only released one studio album, so
how did she accomplish so much?

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:29.480
This is Ms. Lauryn Hill’s story and how
she became one of the most celebrated, influential,

00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:35.160
and respected artists in music history.

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:47.399
First, let’s go way back to before she became
a music icon and a household name.

00:00:47.399 --> 00:00:53.010
In 1987, L. Boogie performed at the famous
live show Amateur Night at the Apollo … and

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:54.010
was boo’d.

00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:55.010
At the tender age of 13, the songbird performed
The Jackson 5’s “Who’s Loving You”

00:00:55.010 --> 00:00:56.010
in front of a crowd, notoriously known for
being relentless critics.

00:00:56.010 --> 00:00:59.180
But as she continued to sing, the crowd eventually
came around and clapped for the singer in

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:04.930
the end, not knowing that they had just witnessed
the beginning of history.

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:17.160
Three years later, Ms. Hill would make another
public appearance as a background dancer in

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:20.000
the music video for “Poor Georgie” by
MC Lyte.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:22.759
But she wouldn’t stay in the background
for long.

00:01:22.759 --> 00:01:23.850
Picture it.

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:28.090
The scene is Columbia High School in Maplewood,
New Jersey.

00:01:28.090 --> 00:01:32.980
Prakazrel Michel, known as "Pras,” approaches
Ms. Hill about joining a group.

00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:36.869
He would later introduce her to Wyclef Jean,
and together they became to be known as the

00:01:36.869 --> 00:01:37.950
Fugees.

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:41.930
As they worked on their debut album Ms. Hill
continued her acting career…

00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:48.180
Which led to her getting cast as Rita Louise
Watson in Sister Act 2 alongside Whoopi Goldberg.

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:54.270
The film served as a breakout film for Ms.
Hill, who also contributed to the soundtrack.

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:58.190
In 1994, the Fugees dropped their first album,
Blunted on Reality.

00:01:58.190 --> 00:02:03.400
The New York Times said the album “concocts
brash, smart raps, drawing on Jamaican dance

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:05.930
hall rhythms as well as American hip-hop.”

00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:08.080
And Ms. Hill was a standout.

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:14.020
She showed the ability to switch between being
a hard-hitting rapper like Ice Cube…

00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:23.800
…to being effortlessly jazzy with her delivery.

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:27.989
But it wasn’t until their sophomore album,
The Score, was released that the Fugees reached

00:02:27.989 --> 00:02:29.599
new levels of success.

00:02:29.599 --> 00:02:32.100
In The Score, they found their own unique
voice.

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:37.280
Pras and Wyclef leaned more into their Caribbean
heritage (Fugees is short for Refugees, after

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:39.970
all) and Ms. Hill brought more of her bluesy
inspiration.

00:02:39.970 --> 00:02:53.260
L. Boogie’s soulful talent is on full display
in their chilling hit single, “Ready or

00:02:53.260 --> 00:02:56.349
Not.”

00:02:56.349 --> 00:03:01.710
Ms. Hill lays her heart bare, with emotional
vocals and even cried while recording, before

00:03:01.710 --> 00:03:15.129
delivering one of her most popular lines…

00:03:15.129 --> 00:03:18.720
But it was Ms. Hill’s cover of “Killing
Me Softly with His Song,” that ended up

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:21.330
being the most popular single from the album.

00:03:21.330 --> 00:03:26.440
In the middle of the hard-hitting 15-track
rap album, Ms. Hill brought new life to the

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:27.440
‘70s song.

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:29.129
With a sample from A Tribe Called Quest…

00:03:29.129 --> 00:03:33.299
… the track’s hip-hop production matched
with Ms. Hill’s raspy-yet-smooth voice and

00:03:33.299 --> 00:03:35.709
made this song an instant classic.

00:03:35.709 --> 00:03:41.189
Just listen to this run…

00:03:41.189 --> 00:03:50.459
The Score went on to win the Grammy Award
for Best Rap Album, making Ms. Hill the first

00:03:50.459 --> 00:03:54.780
woman to win a Grammy in that category.

00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:59.010
A love triangle and creative and financial
disagreements — made it hard for the Fugees

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:00.600
to continue forward as a group.

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:05.150
After Ms. Hill and Wyclef Jean ended their
tumultuous relationship, the Fugees broke

00:04:05.150 --> 00:04:07.420
up, and Ms. Hill went solo.

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:12.360
An early-20-something year old Ms. Hill, started
crafting the early parts of an album in her

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:15.890
living room in her hometown South Orange,
New Jersey.

00:04:15.890 --> 00:04:19.810
Her label reportedly wanted her to release
another album with the Fugees, but Ms. Hill

00:04:19.810 --> 00:04:23.990
forged ahead by recruiting her own team to
help her achieve her vision.

00:04:23.990 --> 00:04:28.310
She told Rolling Stone, “[I wanted to] write
songs that lyrically move me and have the

00:04:28.310 --> 00:04:34.280
integrity of reggae and the knock of hip-hop
and the instrumentation of classic soul.”

00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:39.691
Between label pressure to wrap up the album,
naysayers, filming the movie Restaurant, and

00:04:39.691 --> 00:04:42.810
giving birth to her son, Zion, she got it
done.

00:04:42.810 --> 00:04:47.280
For her debut solo album, The Miseducation
of Lauryn Hill, she drew from her personal

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:52.940
experience to create a raw, emotional cross-genre
album that explored themes of love, Black

00:04:52.940 --> 00:04:56.430
empowerment, social justice, femininity, motherhood,
and more.

00:04:56.430 --> 00:04:58.840
She lays out all of her emotions on the album.

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:03.960
In the soulful ballad “Ex-Factor” she
mourns her relationship with Wyclef Jean…

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:12.370
In “Ex-Factor,” although she’s singing,
her voice bounces on top of the beat in a

00:05:12.370 --> 00:05:19.870
way that embodies a rap flow.

00:05:19.870 --> 00:05:30.180
This sound would also help pave the way for
neo-soul music alongside the artistry of Erykah

00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:33.150
Badu, Floetry, and Jill Scott.

00:05:33.150 --> 00:05:45.810
You can hear Ms. Hill’s Motown soul influence
in songs like Doo Wop (That Thing)

00:05:45.810 --> 00:05:54.180
The track’s hook and riff samples the soul
band the 5th Dimension.

00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:59.330
In Doo Wop, Ms. Hill also showcases her lyrical
flare, proving why Miseducation is seen as

00:05:59.330 --> 00:06:08.850
one of the best rap albums…

00:06:08.850 --> 00:06:14.240
The album is also influenced by Jamaican dancehall
as well as the philosophy and sounds of reggae.

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:18.870
Half of the album was recorded at Bob Marley’s
Studio in Jamaica, and at the time she was

00:06:18.870 --> 00:06:21.900
in a relationship with Marley’s son Rohan.

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:26.830
In the track “Lost Ones” Ms. Hill’s
vocals call back to Sister Nancy’s hit dancehall

00:06:26.830 --> 00:06:39.940
record, “Bam Bam”

00:06:39.940 --> 00:06:44.819
To learn more about the influence of the Miseducation,
I sat down with Nadirah, The founder of The

00:06:44.819 --> 00:06:48.120
Gumbo, a magazine that celebrates Black women
in Hip Hop.

00:06:48.120 --> 00:06:53.190
I think one thing that we don't talk about
a lot, though, at least I love to talk about,

00:06:53.190 --> 00:06:54.370
is the blues.

00:06:54.370 --> 00:06:58.009
…The influence of blues artists in her music.

00:06:58.009 --> 00:07:01.370
And I think if you're hearing the call and
response and old blues songs and you're hearing

00:07:01.370 --> 00:07:05.940
Lauryn sing and you're hearing them respond
with chants and stuff like that and shouting,

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:15.080
especially on songs like To Zion, it's just
so prevalent and it's so obvious there.

00:07:15.080 --> 00:07:26.740
… And when it comes to women and blues and
how they were using, if you think about Ma

00:07:26.740 --> 00:07:31.220
Rainey, you think about Bessie Smith, they
were using the blues to talk about, you know,

00:07:31.220 --> 00:07:36.020
love and relationship issues and family and
heartbreak and all these different things

00:07:36.020 --> 00:07:39.030
that weren't really being done in that way
in music.

00:07:39.030 --> 00:07:44.001
And I think that's what makes it such a great
body of work, is that she is taking all of

00:07:44.001 --> 00:07:48.650
these things and taking all these influences
and really making them her own and just nodding

00:07:48.650 --> 00:07:53.630
to all these different parts of the music
that we have.

00:07:53.630 --> 00:07:55.590
Now let’s talk stats.

00:07:55.590 --> 00:08:00.330
The album, which is her only solo album to
date, would become one of the most highly-acclaimed

00:08:00.330 --> 00:08:02.240
albums of all time.

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:07.110
With over 20 million copies sold worldwide,
it debuted at the No. 1 spot on the Billboard

00:08:07.110 --> 00:08:11.680
200 chart, breaking Madonna’s record for
first-week sales by a female artist.

00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:17.650
During that time, she was also a musical guest
on Saturday Night Live, and had a sold-out

00:08:17.650 --> 00:08:18.650
worldwide tour

00:08:18.650 --> 00:08:23.400
And let’s not forget about her ten Grammy
nominations and five wins including Album

00:08:23.400 --> 00:08:27.020
of the year, making her the first rapper to
win Album of the Year.

00:08:27.020 --> 00:08:32.510
Ms. Hill’s talent earned her the respect
from her predecessors and peers.

00:08:32.510 --> 00:08:36.380
In an interview with Billboard, Grammy-nominated
rapper Rapsody said,

00:08:36.380 --> 00:08:41.649
“She knew how to incorporate melody into
a rhyme so people could sing along with her,

00:08:41.649 --> 00:08:44.730
even as she was rapping about things that
might have been complex.”

00:08:44.730 --> 00:08:48.130
In the same interview, Lizzo noted that “She
set the bar.

00:08:48.130 --> 00:08:52.900
I was always afraid of being a singer, but
then when I heard Lauryn Hill, I was like,

00:08:52.900 --> 00:08:54.850
“Maybe I can do both.”

00:08:54.850 --> 00:08:58.550
Even though the Miseducation of Lauryn Hill
was such a great success, the world is still

00:08:58.550 --> 00:09:01.589
waiting for a sophomore album over 20 years
later.

00:09:01.589 --> 00:09:07.300
Ms. Hill said that lack of support and ideological
differences were the reasons it never happened.

00:09:07.300 --> 00:09:10.339
In an interview with Rolling Stone, Ms. Hill
said

00:09:10.339 --> 00:09:14.740
“No one from my label has ever called me
and asked ‘how can we help you make another

00:09:14.740 --> 00:09:19.810
album…ever [...] With the Miseducation,
there was no precedent.

00:09:19.810 --> 00:09:24.430
I was, for the most part, free to explore,
experiment and express.

00:09:24.430 --> 00:09:30.830
After the Miseducation, there were scores
of tentacled obstructionists, politics, repressing

00:09:30.830 --> 00:09:36.200
agendas, unrealistic expectations, and saboteurs
everywhere.”

00:09:36.200 --> 00:09:41.170
With only one solo album, some think that
Ms. Hill’s legacy stops with Miseducation,

00:09:41.170 --> 00:09:43.860
but that’s not true.

00:09:43.860 --> 00:09:49.079
In 2002, she released an MTV Unplugged album,
which includes a performance of “Mystery

00:09:49.079 --> 00:10:04.350
of Iniquity” that was later sampled in Kanye
West’s “All Falls Down.”

00:10:04.350 --> 00:10:10.149
Ms. Hill also wrote and produced songs for
legends like Aretha Franklin, Mary J. Blige,

00:10:10.149 --> 00:10:13.930
CeCe Winans, Whitney Houston, and Santana.

00:10:13.930 --> 00:10:17.010
Why do you think Miss Lauryn Hill has only
released one album?

00:10:17.010 --> 00:10:18.580
Everyone talks about this so much.

00:10:18.580 --> 00:10:24.350
And also, I couldn't imagine creating something
like The Miseducation of Lauryn Hill, Lauryn

00:10:24.350 --> 00:10:30.709
Hill, and then trying to follow it up with
all that pressure on me because, one, I gave

00:10:30.709 --> 00:10:31.839
y'all something really good.

00:10:31.839 --> 00:10:33.790
I also did the live album.

00:10:33.790 --> 00:10:43.079
I haven't completely like disappeared or whatever,
but I'm also a human being.

00:10:43.079 --> 00:10:46.880
Ms. Hill continues to tour and performs new
renditions of her songs to keep it fresh for

00:10:46.880 --> 00:10:49.040
her audiences — and herself.

00:10:49.040 --> 00:10:53.760
Since the Fugees broke up in 1997, they’ve
performed together a few times, and there’s

00:10:53.760 --> 00:10:55.050
been talk of another reunion.

00:10:55.050 --> 00:10:58.540
And while she draws criticism for only releasing
one studio album…

00:10:58.540 --> 00:11:03.540
her influence can be heard in the music of
today’s chart toppers like Beyoncé, Adele,

00:11:03.540 --> 00:11:18.360
J. Cole, Drake, and Cardi B…

00:11:18.360 --> 00:11:23.910
I feel like a lot of the young artists, especially
because I'm an R&amp;B, especially R&amp;B.

00:11:23.910 --> 00:11:26.750
Yeah, take a lot of Lauryn influence.

00:11:26.750 --> 00:11:27.750
They definitely do.

00:11:27.750 --> 00:11:28.750
They definitely do.

00:11:28.750 --> 00:11:33.550
And even I feel like a lot of the women who
rap now, too, if you ask them, you know, who

00:11:33.550 --> 00:11:36.920
are your favorite almost all the time, they
say Lauryn Hill.

00:11:36.920 --> 00:11:40.060
And I'm just like, this is a testament to
her impact.

00:11:40.060 --> 00:11:46.959
Good night you guys, thank you very much.

00:11:46.959 --> 00:11:51.000
Before you go, I want to let you know about
Fight The Power: How Hip-Hop Changed the World.

00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:56.269
A new PBS series hosted by Hip-Hop legend
Chuck D. It’s about how Hip-Hop became a

00:11:56.269 --> 00:11:59.300
global movement that spoke truth to power.

00:11:59.300 --> 00:12:05.720
Check out the link in the description below
and let them know Sound Field sent you.

